{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793906098,"nonce":"nbGtgFWu2NwZNKaEluZ3r0ir3T0=","title":"Inliner reorders module functions even when it rejects every call","message":"At 6d9f9ba3dca76c5663b3b69db3c3e4269c4ea5b7, construct a module with a no_inline callee followed by a caller. inline::run_module reports inlined=0 but reverses function insertion order. run_module_with removes optimizing functions sequentially with shift_remove_full; each removal records its index after earlier removals, so multiple functions record index 0. Reinsertion by these shifted indices does not recover original order. Individual bodies remain unchanged.\n\nFound while adding allocation scaling coverage in the inliner setup work. Test now compares bodies by ID so it can isolate rejected-argument copies; no production ordering change is included in that performance experiment. Reproduce with two optimizing functions in insertion order [callee,caller] and no accepted inlines. Correctness/codegen-order consequences need separate assessment before changing it.","files":null}]}